80, the two top milers in the world were both from Great Britain – " [:a {:href "/athletes/69364"} "Steve Ovett"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/68969"} "Sebastian Coe"] ". Ovett had won the 1978 European Championship. Coe came to the fore in the summer of 1979 when he broke the world records for 800 metres, 1,500 metres, and the mile. At the Dubai Golden Mile on 17 July, he ran 3:48.95 to break " [:a {:href "/athletes/74363"} "John Walker"] "’s mile record. A few weeks later, at the Weltklasse Meet in Zürich, he ran 3:32.03 to better " [:a {:href "/athletes/76507"} "Filbert Bayi"] "’s record for 1,500 metres. But Ovett had been ranked #1 in the world in 1977-78 and he fought back, posting times of 3:49.57 and 3:32.11. Then on 21 May 1980, at the Crystal Palace in London, Ovett broke Coe’s world mile record, running 3:48.8. And in early June, at Bislett Stadium in Oslo, he ran 3:32.09 for 1,500 metres. In that era both his mark and Coe’s world record of 3:32.03 were rounded up to 3:32.1 and Ovett had another world record."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Moskva Olympics were sorely affected by the American-led boycott, and two runners who were missed were " [:a {:href "/athletes/74363"} "John Walker"] ", the defending champion, and American " [:a {:href "/athletes/79011"} "Steve Scott"] ". But the British Olympic Association defied their government and Great Britain entered a team, allowing both Coe and Ovett to compete. Earlier in the Olympics, they both ran the 800, where Coe was considered the stronger runner because of his greater speed. But Ovett won that race in a slight upset, Coe getting the silver medal. In the 1,500 final, the pace was absurdly slow, passing 800 in 2:04.9. The East German " [:a {:href "/athletes/69896"} "Jürgen Straub"] " then began a sustained kick, trying to draw out the field. It played into Coe’s hands, who could maintain high speed longer than anyone. He pulled up to Straub on the final curve and passed him at the start of the homestretch, holding him off in the straight, with Ovett finishing third."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "254"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/68969"} "Seb Coe"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "3:40.1 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "3:39.4 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "3:38.4 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "338"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/69896"} "Jürgen Straub"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GDR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GDR.png"}] "GDR"]] [:td {} "3:37.0 (2 h4)"] [:td {} "3:39.4 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "3:38.8 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "279"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/69364"} "Steve Ovett"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "3:36.8 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "3:43.1 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "3:39.0 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "296"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/69766"} "Andreas Busse"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GDR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GDR.png"}] "GDR"]] [:td {} "3:44.3 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "3:43.5 (3 h1)"] [:td {} "3:40.2 (4)"] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td